 experience in their daily lives as a as  a general rule are  the approaches that are more likely or  have worked more effectively  about as you mentioned with police it's  higher good apples rather than convert  it does that apply universally  yeah you you sort of need to do all of  the above to be honest because  the problem is not just the individual  it's also the system right i mean if you  have a good person who goes into a  rotten police department they're gonna  probably behave in a rotten way and one  of the more unusual things that i did  that might be of interest to your  viewers and listeners is i took a ski  lesson with paul bremmer who ran iraq in  2003 and now he's a ski instructor in  vermont believe it or not so he's an  interesting case study because you know  a lot of people see him as the villain  and i understand completely why i mean  what he did in 2003 you know the the  record speaks for itself but what's  interesting about him is that he was  previously an ambassador to malawi and  norway and it appears that he did a very  good job i mean across most people think  that he was really effective  and sort of had lots of integrity in  that position and so on and then he goes  into iraq where everything is broken and  he's basically you know a de facto  viceroy of a dictatorship and all of a  sudden everything he has to do is these  awful decisions with morally dubious  consequences and so on and so i think  one of the things that we also have to  acknowledge it's not to absolve people  who do bad things in positions of power  it's that it's more complicated  sometimes than it initially appears and  that the system also needs our attention  because  you know i i like to think that i would  behave well no matter what but you know  you see the evidence from around the  world and you you take people and put  them in new systems and they do behave  in different ways there's a study of  bankers in italy where southern italy is  known as being more corrupt than  northern italy yes and the employees who  move between the same bank but different  branches start embezzling at higher  rates if they're in the south so you  know there's things like that where we  can see that it's both the individual  and the system that need our attention  um  i i this may seem like a sort of  divergence but but i i think it it could  be an interesting one the role of power  within uh other species within within  the animal kingdom um what do we know  that mirrors homo sapiens and what what  differences are there  yeah it's a great question it's one of  the most enjoyable parts of the research  because you know i'm a political  scientist so i don't spend a lot of time  with people who study hyenas and bees  and primates and so on but i did for the  book yeah and it was really really  fascinating primates are a great window  for us because it's  similar in terms of some of the brain  functions and some social structures  without the culture right and so  you can look at how power affects  individuals there's an amazing study i i  interviewed a professor and doctor  out in north carolina who  looks at hierarchy and drug addiction in  macaque monkeys  and one of the craziest things is that  when you basically give monkeys you put  them in groups of four they instantly  form a hierarchy from one to four  and then you offer them the choice  between cocaine where they push a button  and they get intravenous cocaine or  banana pellets they push a different  button and they get banana pellets and  what's what's amazing about this is that  every time you run this study the top  two monkeys the ones that are on the top  of the hierarchy don't take the cocaine  they take the banana pellets the ones  who are on the bottom  basically self-medicate  and this happens even if the alpha male  in this group gets reassigned to another  group of four and then ends up on the  bottom all of a sudden the exact same  thing happens so it doesn't matter about  the individual i also looked at  corruption in believe it or not bees and  wasps and it's an amazing story about  systems and individual behavior i won't  go into the whole story but basically  the the gist of it is the way the hive  is set up between different kinds of  species of wasps whether it's  transparent or not whether it's easy to  detect wrongdoing or not by what are  actually called police wasps or police  bees  that has a massive massive effect on how  selfish the behavior of the individual  wasp or b is  and i think these lessons that we have  from the animal kingdom are actually  quite instructive because  most of the time when we talk about  these problems we're talking about them  within the silos of democrats and  republicans yeah and sometimes it's  worth stripping that away and saying  okay there's some universal lessons that  we can learn and we might as well draw  them from wherever we can even if it's  from an unexpected place that's super  interesting yeah and and uh not that  long ago i read jared diamond's book the  third chimpanzee which also talks a lot  about um sort of the parts that are  analogous and parts that are not between  humans and and chimpanzees and there's  uh there's a lot of different layers to  that that you don't really see if the  focus is like american partisan politics  i guess is the point that you're making  yeah i mean i i think that there's some  some problems in society that actually  can be solved right i mean i think we  focus on so much stuff that is the  partisan battle of the day which makes  complete sense yeah but we can actually  make systems of power behave better for  all of us and that requires some  outside-the-box thinking that might have  reference points that aren't our typical  culture wars right you know it's hard to  get worked up about uh you know somebody  making a point about  wasp corruption for example it's not  exactly gonna rile you up in the same  way  and so on so i i think we can draw these  lessons the other point that i make by  the way is i as part of the research  this book i interviewed i met with lots  of really awful people i i met with lots  of former despots war criminals all  sorts  around the world and one of the points  that i often make is you would find it  super weird if somebody who was an  expert on hyenas  never  encountered a hyena never studied one  never went up to one and observed it and  yet there's a lot of stuff in politics  where these monsters among us still are  living breathing and willing to talk to  people so  i went to study them to figure out what  makes them tick and what power does to  them and i think we need to do more of  that because these problems are  preventable but we have to sort of strip  away some of the the the sort of  preconceptions or  uh revulsion that we have and start to  think okay we're stuck with some of  these people how can we make them behave  better how can we replace them how can  we make sure that better people follow  them and that requires  some sort of outside the partisan frame  thinking i think  the book is corruptable who gets power  and how it changes us we've been  speaking with the book's author brian  class brian so great uh talking about  this and always really appreciate your  time  thanks david thanks for having me
 

